Unity USD Toolkit patch to OpenUSD v26.05 (commit 2095fafafd033fa23386d7ec6d58c7cc33974518), applied
after openusd-26.05-file-format-hardening.patch: counts in binary layers (.usdc, the crate format)
checked before anything they size is allocated.

Applied by Native~/build_openusd.py to a worktree of the pinned commit, never to the clone itself;
its SHA-256 is pinned in Native~/dependency-sources/<platform>.tsv. Every change is marked
"Unity USD Toolkit" in the source. The crate reader took counts from the file -- array lengths, the
token, field, spec and path tables, list and dictionary sizes -- and allocated for them before the
read that would have failed; resize() value-initializes, so the memory was committed, not just
reserved. One changed byte in a valid file made it take gigabytes (on Linux and macOS, which
overcommit, enough to exhaust the machine), and a compressed integer array whose data was short
still "succeeded": the reader kept an array of the claimed length, decoded from uninitialized
memory. Found by fuzzing the Windows payload (8,000 mutated .usdc files under a 1 GB cap: 364
ran out of memory, 12 of them by filling it); with this patch none of them, nor 20,000 further
cases, take more than ~100 MB, and files OpenUSD writes read exactly as before.

crateFile.cpp:
1. The byte streams report their length, and _Reader::CheckCount refuses a count that cannot fit
   in the bytes left (SdfReadOutOfBoundsError, which the callers already turn into a corrupt-file
   error): uncompressed arrays (also keeping sizeof(T) * size from overflowing into a small range
   that passed the zero-copy check), vectors, list ops, dictionaries, time-sample value counts, the
   token and field-value byte counts.
2. Compressed integers (_CompressedIntsReader) are read in two steps: Prepare reads and
   decompresses them and checks the data holds the count claimed; only then is the destination
   allocated, and Decode fills it. Used by compressed int and float arrays and by the fields,
   field sets, specs and paths tables. A float array's lookup-table index is checked.
3. Tokens: the decompressed size is at most 256 times the compressed one, decompression has to
   produce all of it, and the token count cannot exceed its bytes. Field value reps have to fill
   their table.
4. Paths: the total cannot exceed the encoded paths plus the empty one, and the encoded count is
   checked before _paths is allocated. The jumps are walked once before the paths are built and
   must reach each entry at most once: damaged jumps that reached one entry two ways made
   _BuildDecompressedPathsImpl spawn tasks without bound and write one SdfPath from several
   threads.
5. Values nest at most 256 deep (the recursion guard's size): deeper nesting overflowed the stack.
6. The table of contents: every section has to lie inside the file and have a terminated name.
   Their ranges went unchecked to the prefetch (on Linux and macOS, posix_madvise over memory
   outside the mapping) and, when a layer is saved over itself, to new char[size] for sections
   this version does not know; an unterminated name sent the section lookup's strcmp past it.
7. Time samples have to hold one value per time, as the writer stores them. The values are read
   by index into the times, so a damaged file with more times than values read what follows the
   values as value reps -- another attribute's samples, of any type -- without an error.

integerCoding.{h,cpp}:
8. Decoding checks, from the codes, that the decompressed bytes hold numInts integers before it
   reads them (it read past what was decompressed), and DecompressToWorkingSpace /
   DecodeFromWorkingSpace split the two steps for item 2.

